  "title": "The wisdom books",
  "concepts": [
   "Fear of the LORD",
   "Proverbs, not promises",
   "Under the sun",
   "Love as strong as death"
  ],
  "motto": [
   "Silver from the mine.",
   "Wisdom from God."
  ],
  "lead": [
   "Four books in the middle of the Old Testament teach a way of living more than they tell a story: Job, Proverbs, Ecclesiastes and the Song of Songs. Christians call them the wisdom books. Wisdom here does not mean being clever. It means the skill of living well in God’s world, before God. This study walks all four and teaches one habit: before you ask what a line promises, ask who is speaking and what kind of saying it is."
  ],
  "habit": [
   "These four books do not all speak the same way. A reader who treats every line as a promise will get hurt, or hurt someone else. In Job, three friends talk for chapters about why people suffer, and at the end God tells them, “you have not spoken about Me accurately” (",
   {
    "r": "JOB.42.7",
    "t": "Job 42:7"
   },
   "). Their words are in the Bible, and the Bible reports them as wrong. In Proverbs, two sayings stand side by side and tell you to do opposite things (",
   {
    "r": "PRO.26.4-5",
    "t": "Proverbs 26:4–5"
   },
   "). Ecclesiastes keeps saying that what it describes happens “under the sun” (",
   {
    "r": "ECC.1.3",
    "t": "Ecclesiastes 1:3"
   },
   "). The Song of Songs is a love poem. A report of a debate, a proverb, a searching question and a song each ask to be read in their own way. So ask first: who is speaking here, and what kind of saying is this?"
  ],
  "setting": [
   "In the Hebrew Bible these books stand in its third part, the Writings, after the Law and the Prophets. Jewish communities read two of them aloud each year among five short scrolls: the Song of Songs at Passover, the spring festival remembering the rescue from Egypt, and Ecclesiastes at Sukkot, the autumn harvest festival when families live for a week in temporary shelters. Christians have read all four as God’s word from the beginning, and the New Testament quotes them."
  ],
  "selection": [
   "This is a selection, not a census. The study walks ",
   {
    "r": "JOB.28",
    "t": "Job 28"
   },
   " and ",
   {
    "r": "JOB.38-42",
    "t": "Job 38–42"
   },
   "; ",
   {
    "r": "PRO.1-9",
    "t": "Proverbs 1–9"
   },
   " and a handful of sayings from chapters 10, 16, 22 and 26; ",
   {
    "r": "ECC.1-3",
    "t": "Ecclesiastes 1–3"
   },
   " and 12; and the Song of Songs as a whole. It does not walk the long debate in ",
   {
    "r": "JOB.3-27",
    "t": "Job 3–27"
   },
   " and 29–37, most of ",
   {
    "r": "PRO.11-31",
    "t": "Proverbs 11–31"
   },
   ", or ",
   {
    "r": "ECC.4-11",
    "t": "Ecclesiastes 4–11"
   },
   ". The Psalms are often counted among the wisdom writings too; they have their own study. Catholic and Orthodox Bibles add two further wisdom books, the Wisdom of Solomon and Sirach, which Baptists, with other Protestants, do not count as Scripture. This study reads Scripture; it is not medical, financial or professional guidance."
  ],
  "close": {
   "title": "Reading the wisdom books",
   "way": [
    "Here is the habit to carry to the next page of these books. Ask who is speaking: Job, a friend, God, a father, Wisdom, Folly, the Teacher, the bride. Ask what kind of saying it is: a report, a proverb, a searching question, a song. Ask what it does not promise. Then ask where it points, and you will usually find the same place: the fear of the Lord, and turning from evil."
   ],
   "close": [
    "Job learned that wisdom belongs to God, and met God without being told why. Proverbs showed what reverence looks like at home, at work and in the street. Ecclesiastes told the truth about death and limits, and still found gifts from God’s hand. The Song sang that love is stronger than death. Jesus once said that someone greater than Solomon was standing in front of the crowd (",
    {
     "r": "MAT.12.42",
     "t": "Matthew 12:42"
    },
    "). The wisdom these books seek is finally found in Him."
   ],
   "invite": [
    "If you have not yet trusted Christ, the gospel, the good news, is that God gives what none of us can dig up or buy. Christ died for our sins and rose again, and God forgives and receives everyone who turns from sin and trusts Him. “There is no salvation apart from personal faith in Jesus Christ as Lord” (article IV, salvation). Read more in ",
    {
     "s": "../the-good-news-directions/",
     "t": "The good news: how a person is made right with God"
    },
    "."
   ],
   "question": [
    "Which kind of saying have you been reading as a promise, and what is it really asking of you?"
   ],
   "again": [
    "Read the passage again before you answer."
   ],
   "passage": "JOB.28.20-28"
  }
